===persian===

جانا... دوباره نگاه کن
نگاه کردن دیدن نیست...
از ابر تا باران...
یک قلم فاصله است...
یک نفس آبی‌تر...
مهتاب خاموش می‌شود.
عمری گذشت... تا
به چشمِ خود دیدم...
چه مشکل است
که با چشم خود ببینم.
از هر مژه به ساحلِ دیدن راهی‌ست...
چه مرزِ حساسی...
باریک و تاریک...
با کفش‌های شوق،
از «مسیر نبضِ عناصر»*
عبور کن...
در معبری چنین...
باید چراغ رنگ به دست بگیریم
و در تاریک‌ترین لحظه
با نگاهی تَر
و ذکر «دوستت دارم»
به دنبال پیری بگردیم...
و از او چیزی بپرسیم...
هر کدام مطلب خود را...
در چنین حالی
من از او می‌پرسم...
اِی پیر...
من سال‌هاست در مسیرِ خاکستری‌ام...
کِی می‌توانم یک قطره بیشتر
با سرخ نقاشی کنم...؟
جانا... تو چه می‌پرسی؟...
بهار ۱۳۶۹

*Footnote at the foot of the page:* ‎* سهراب سپهری

===machine===

My love... look again —
looking is not seeing...
From cloud to rain...
is one brushstroke's distance...
one breath bluer...
and the moonlight goes out.
A lifetime passed... before
I saw with my own eyes...
how hard it is
to see with my own eyes.
From every eyelash to the shore of seeing there is a road...
what a delicate border...
narrow, and dark...
With the shoes of longing,
pass through
"the path of the pulse of the elements"*...
On a crossing like this...
we must take up the lamp of color
and in the darkest moment,
with a wet gaze
and the invocation "I love you,"
go looking for some old man...
and ask him something...
each of us his own question...
In such a state
I ask him...
O old man...
For years now I have been on my gray road...
When will I be able to paint
one drop more in red...?
My love... and what do you ask?...
Spring 1369

*Footnote:* * Sohrab Sepehri

===lantern===

My love... look again —
looking is not seeing...
From cloud to rain
is one brushstroke's distance...
one breath bluer,
and the moonlight goes out.
A lifetime went by... before
I saw with my own eyes
how hard it is
to see with my own eyes.
From every eyelash there is a road to the shore of seeing...
what a delicate border,
narrow, and dark...
In the shoes of longing,
cross over by way of
"the path of the pulse of the elements"*...
On a crossing like that
we have to take up the lamp of colour,
and at the darkest moment,
with a wet gaze
and the invocation "I love you,"
go looking for some old man...
and ask him something...

each of us his own question...
In such a state
I ask him:
Old man...
for years now I have been on my gray road...
When will I be able to paint
one drop more in red...?
My love... and you — what do you ask?...

Spring 1369

===translation===

===footnotes===

* سهراب سپهری — Sohrab Sepehri (1928–1980), from whom the quoted phrase
«مسیر نبضِ عناصر», *the path of the pulse of the elements*, is taken. Sepehri was a painter as well
as a poet, which is why a poem addressed to young painters reaches for him: he is the precedent for
the whole double life this book is about.

ذکر is not simply "saying" — it is the Sufi's repeated invocation, the practice of holding a name
in the mouth until it changes you. Putting "I love you" in that slot is the poem's boldest move,
and the English keeps "invocation" rather than smoothing it.

And the question the old man is asked is the one the book has been circling for twenty pages: after
a lifetime on the gray road — the grays he says outright he loves on page 76, the gray that is red
and green cancelling on page 88 — when will he be allowed to paint *one drop more in red*. It is
the same drop his daughter Sara spilled beside the mute bird on page 84.

